d":18,"play_caller":"Lou Anarumo"},{"season":2021,"team":"CAR","distance":0.766,"shared":18,"play_caller":null},{"season":2019,"team":"ATL","distance":0.8068,"shared":18,"play_caller":"Dan Quinn"}]},"overview":{"overview":"The Browns went 5-12 in 2025 and were outscored 379 to 279. The defense did most of the work, finishing 5th in EPA allowed while the offense ranked 31st in EPA per play. Their most distinctive traits were the highest 12 personnel usage and the highest eight man box rate in the league.","offense":"The Browns offense struggled, ranking 25th or worse in all five main efficiency measures. Their run and pass mix stayed close to what game situations called for. Schematically, they stood out for the highest 12 personnel usage and a number of backs in the backfield that was among the lowest in the league. Shedeur Sanders ran the passing game, with Harold Fannin Jr. leading the team in targets.","defense":"The Browns had one of the best defenses in the league, ranking 5th or better in three of the five main efficiency measures. Its strongest area was pressure rate, where it ranked 1st, and its weakest was takeaways per game, where it ranked 22nd. Myles Garrett was as productive as expected, but the bigger surprise was Maliek Collins, who had 6.5 sacks, after not being a regular on this defense last season. Schematically, they had the highest eight man box rate in the league.","special":"The Browns were about average in how often they went for it on fourth down. Their kickers made 89% of field goals, which was well above average. Their salary cap was spread across positions in a fairly typical way. The offense also turned over more of its roster than most, with 44% of its snaps going to players who were on the team the year before.","players":"Shedeur Sanders played nearly every snap at quarterback and threw for 1,400 yards. Targets were spread around, with Harold Fannin Jr. and Jerry Jeudy leading a group where nobody took more than a fifth of the looks. Quinshon Judkins handled most of the work in the backfield with 61% of the carries. On defense, Myles Garrett led the team with 23 sacks and Devin Bush Jr. picked off 3 passes.","coaching":"Kevin Stefanski called the offense, and the defensive play caller for this season is not in the coaching data. Their offense looked most like the 2025 Raiders under Chip Kelly and the 2023 Patriots. Their defense looked most like the 2019 and 2020 Eagles.","trends":"The offense went from 32nd in EPA per play in 2024 to 31st in 2025, about where it was. The defense moved from 18th in EPA allowed per play in 2024 to 5th in 2025, a big step forward. The biggest change in approach was number of receivers on the field, which went from 2.77 to 2.33.","notes":"Every data source is available for 2025. This season uses the newer formation labels, which group singleback, I formation and empty sets together as under center, so those older formation rates are not shown."}}